WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.826
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Cambodia Grade twelve English.

2
00:00:06.126 --> 00:00:09.513
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.813 --> 00:00:17.469
Unit nine: Modals of Deduction and Past Modals. Today you will guess about the present and past with must, might and can't.

4
00:00:17.769 --> 00:00:20.444
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.714
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:26.598
Deduction.

7
00:00:27.583 --> 00:00:30.105
Deduction means a conclusion from clues.

8
00:00:30.717 --> 00:00:32.229
That is a good deduction.

9
00:00:32.817 --> 00:00:35.608
Your turn. Say: Deduction.

10
00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:39.398
Deduction.

11
00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:41.081
Must.

12
00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:43.781
Must means almost certain.

13
00:00:44.383 --> 00:00:45.651
He must be at home.

14
00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:48.380
Your turn. Say: Must.

15
00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:52.188
Must.

16
00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:53.789
Might.

17
00:00:54.783 --> 00:00:56.226
Might means possible.

18
00:00:56.817 --> 00:00:58.108
She might be late.

19
00:00:58.717 --> 00:01:00.707
Your turn. Say: Might.

20
00:01:03.567 --> 00:01:04.359
Might.

21
00:01:05.117 --> 00:01:05.816
Can't.

22
00:01:06.817 --> 00:01:08.375
Can't means impossible.

23
00:01:08.983 --> 00:01:10.217
That can't be true.

24
00:01:10.817 --> 00:01:12.889
Your turn. Say: Can't.

25
00:01:15.733 --> 00:01:16.618
Can't.

26
00:01:17.383 --> 00:01:18.315
Should have.

27
00:01:19.317 --> 00:01:21.514
Should have means advice about the past.

28
00:01:22.117 --> 00:01:23.536
You should have called.

29
00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:26.942
Your turn. Say: Should have.

30
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:30.813
Should have.

31
00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:32.470
Could have.

32
00:01:33.483 --> 00:01:35.587
Could have means a past possibility.

33
00:01:36.183 --> 00:01:37.428
I could have won.

34
00:01:38.017 --> 00:01:40.576
Your turn. Say: Could have.

35
00:01:43.433 --> 00:01:44.434
Could have.

36
00:01:45.183 --> 00:01:46.289
Evidence.

37
00:01:47.283 --> 00:01:49.736
Evidence means facts that give support.

38
00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:51.955
There is clear evidence.

39
00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:54.889
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

40
00:01:57.733 --> 00:01:58.781
Evidence.

41
00:01:59.517 --> 00:02:00.332
Certain.

42
00:02:01.317 --> 00:02:02.794
Certain means sure.

43
00:02:03.383 --> 00:02:04.547
I am certain.

44
00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:07.373
Your turn. Say: Certain.

45
00:02:10.233 --> 00:02:11.072
Certain.

46
00:02:11.867 --> 00:02:15.367
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:16.217 --> 00:02:18.309
Visal, how do I make deductions?

48
00:02:19.417 --> 00:02:30.231
Use must when you are almost sure, might or may when it is possible, and can't when it is impossible. The lights are on, so he must be at home.

49
00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:32.643
How about the past?

50
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:42.298
Use modal + have + past participle: She must have left early, He might have forgotten, They can't have arrived yet.

51
00:02:43.383 --> 00:02:45.360
What about criticism and advice?

52
00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:58.602
Should have shows a past mistake: You should have studied. Could have shows an unused chance: I could have passed. Needn't have means it was not necessary.

53
00:02:59.767 --> 00:03:01.313
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:06.054
Grammar focus: modal plus have plus past participle.

55
00:03:06.354 --> 00:03:07.529
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:08.217 --> 00:03:09.589
He must be at home.

57
00:03:10.483 --> 00:03:11.995
She must have left early.

58
00:03:12.933 --> 00:03:21.147
Use must for strong certainty, might for possibility and can't for impossibility. For the past, use modal + have + past participle.

59
00:03:21.883 --> 00:03:25.186
Be careful! Don't say: She must left early.

60
00:03:27.133 --> 00:03:29.121
Say: She must have left early.

61
00:03:29.421 --> 00:03:31.630
modal + have + V3.

62
00:03:32.433 --> 00:03:34.958
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:35.767 --> 00:03:36.884
Who Took It?.

64
00:03:37.184 --> 00:03:40.356
The classroom window is open and the bag is gone.

65
00:03:41.367 --> 00:03:42.925
Someone must have taken it.

66
00:03:43.933 --> 00:03:47.013
It can't have been the teacher because she was in a meeting.

67
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.696
It might have been a student from another class.

68
00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:53.073
They should have locked the door.

69
00:03:54.167 --> 00:03:58.064
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:03:58.917 --> 00:04:01.218
Almost certain: He blank be at home.

71
00:04:01.518 --> 00:04:04.603
A, must. B, can't. C, needn't.

72
00:04:08.167 --> 00:04:09.957
The answer is A: must.

73
00:04:11.117 --> 00:04:13.557
Impossible: That blank be true.

74
00:04:13.857 --> 00:04:16.814
A, should. B, can't. C, must.

75
00:04:20.367 --> 00:04:22.529
The answer is B: can't.

76
00:04:23.683 --> 00:04:26.414
Past deduction: She must blank left.

77
00:04:26.714 --> 00:04:29.764
A, has. B, had. C, have.

78
00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:34.928
The answer is C: have.

79
00:04:36.083 --> 00:04:39.325
Advice about the past: You should blank called.

80
00:04:39.625 --> 00:04:42.419
A, have. B, be. C, to.

81
00:04:45.967 --> 00:04:48.129
The answer is A: have.

82
00:04:49.283 --> 00:04:50.424
What was gone?

83
00:04:50.724 --> 00:04:54.261
A, the window. B, the bag. C, the door.

84
00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:59.997
The answer is B: the bag.

85
00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.201
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:06.446
What might have happened?

87
00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:16.692
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:16.992 --> 00:05:25.116
The bus might have broken down. Perhaps there was heavy traffic. He can't have forgotten because he is always on time.

89
00:05:25.416 --> 00:05:27.520
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:05:28.417 --> 00:05:33.809
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and modal plus have plus past participle.

91
00:05:34.109 --> 00:05:39.083
Today's value is analytical thinking. Look for the pattern underneath the surface.

92
00:05:39.383 --> 00:05:41.722
See you in Unit ten. Goodbye!

93
00:05:42.022 --> 00:05:45.603
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

